@page "/download"
@using RavenNest.Blazor.Services.Models
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.WebUtilities
@using RavenNest.Blazor.Services
@inject NavigationManager NavigationManager
@inject AuthService AuthService

<div class="top-section">
    <div class="hero-text">
        <h1>Get started with Ravenfall</h1>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="white-section-break"></div>
<div class="page-content">

    <div class="section">
        <p class="rf-lead">
            Ravenfall runs on your machine and your viewers play it from chat. Download the client,
            set a password, and type !join in your own channel.
        </p>

        <div class="rf-downloads">
            <div class="rf-download">
                <h3 class="rf-download__title"><i class="fa-brands fa-windows" aria-hidden="true"></i> Windows x64</h3>
                <p class="rf-download__body">The supported build, with automatic updates.</p>
                <a href="/download/latest" class="rf-btn rf-btn--primary">
                    <i class="fa-solid fa-download" aria-hidden="true"></i> Download for Windows
                </a>
            </div>

            <div class="rf-download">
                <h3 class="rf-download__title">
                    <i class="fa-brands fa-linux" aria-hidden="true"></i> Linux x86/x64
                    <span class="rf-badge rf-badge--warn">Experimental</span>
                </h3>
                <p class="rf-download__body">
                    Not officially supported and it has no working auto update. The link may not
                    work if the current version has no successful Linux build.
                </p>
                <a href="/download/linux" class="rf-btn">
                    <i class="fa-solid fa-download" aria-hidden="true"></i> Download for Linux
                </a>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
        <h1>System requirements</h1>
        @*
            Was a bullet list of three lines that each had to carry their own label. A facts list
            is the shape of a label and a value, and it puts the three where they can be compared
            against what you have.
        *@
        <div class="rf-prose">
            <dl class="rf-facts">
                <dt>Operating system</dt>
                <dd>64 bit Windows 10 or later. Linux is untested.</dd>

                <dt>Processor</dt>
                <dd>An i5-6700 or equivalent, with at least four cores.</dd>

                <dt>Graphics</dt>
                <dd>A GTX 970 or equivalent. A dedicated card rather than integrated.</dd>
            </dl>

            <p class="rf-meta">
                These are estimates for about 25 players. More players than that wants more
                machine. Tell us what you are running on
                <a href="https://discord.gg/gtfDts7" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Discord</a> and
                we will sharpen these numbers.
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
        <h1>Setting it up</h1>
        <div class="rf-prose">
            <ol class="rf-steps">
                <li>
                    <b>Unzip the client.</b> Use <a href="https://www.7-zip.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">7-Zip</a>
                    or similar and extract it into a folder of its own.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <b>Set a password.</b> The client cannot sign in with Twitch, so it needs one:
                    set it on the <a href="/password">password page</a>.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <b>Log in.</b> Open the client and sign in with your Twitch username and that
                    password. You should arrive in the village on Home island.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <b>Join from chat.</b> Type <span class="rf-cmd">!join</span> in your own
                    channel. RavenfallOfficial replies and your character appears in the client.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <b>Leave fullscreen</b> with Alt and Enter if you would rather it ran in a
                    window.
                </li>
            </ol>

            <p class="rf-meta">
                Two factor authentication on your Twitch account is only needed if you intend to
                run your own bot rather than the official one.
                <a href="https://help.twitch.tv/s/article/two-factor-authentication?language=en_US" target="_blank" rel="noopener">How to set it up</a>.
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
        <h1>Worth knowing</h1>
        <div class="rf-prose">
            <ul class="rf-tips">
                <li><b>The client has to be running</b> for characters to earn experience, even when your stream is offline.</li>
                <li><b>It does not have to be on screen.</b> Plenty of streamers run it offscreen and bring it up for raids and dungeons.</li>
                <li><b>Viewers can play while you are offline</b> by typing in <span class="rf-cmd">twitch.tv/popout/[username]/chat</span>.</li>
                <li><b>Free camera is Ctrl and Tab</b>, which is what you need to set town bonuses.</li>
                <li><b>Town plots boost a skill</b> for everyone playing on your stream. Assign them in free camera at the Home village, or from your <a href="/town">town page</a>.</li>
                <li><b>Do not spread your town too thin.</b> On a new server, putting everything into one skill is usually better than a little of each.</li>
                <li><b>The music is public domain</b>, so you are free to use it on stream.</li>
                <li><b>If the player list size resets</b> after a restart, open the UI settings and it comes back.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
        <p class="rf-meta">
            The <a href="https://wiki.ravenfall.stream/index.php/Streamer_Setup_Guide" target="_blank" rel="noopener">community wiki setup guide</a>
            goes further than this page and is kept up to date by players.
        </p>
    </div>

</div>
